# Session Initialization Protocol

Do not add hardcoded tool names, MCP server names, or Context7 library IDs; discover these at runtime.

At the start of every new Cursor session, dynamically discover and catalogue available resources before beginning any task. Never rely on hardcoded lists -- always derive the catalogue from runtime configuration so it stays current as servers/plugins change.

## 1. Discover MCP Servers

- Use Cursor MCP settings (Settings > MCP) to see configured servers and their connection status. If the project has an `mcps/` directory with tool descriptors, read that as well.
- For each server, note its connection status (active, failed, disconnected)
- List the tools each server exposes (from MCP settings or from each server's `tools/` subfolder under `mcps/` if present)
- Flag any servers that are FAILED so they can be triaged

## 2. Discover Built-in Tools

- Enumerate the tools available in the current Cursor agent context (they are injected by the system prompt -- do not maintain a static list here)
- Note which subagent types are available via the Task tool

## 3. Discover Plugins and Skills

- Glob `~/.cursor/skills-cursor/*/SKILL.md` to find installed Cursor skills
- Glob `~/.codex/skills/*/SKILL.md` to find installed Codex skills
- Glob `agents/*.md` in the workspace for project-level agents
- Glob `commands/*.md` in the workspace for project-level commands

## 4. Verify Context7 Documentation Access

- Call `resolve-library-id` for key topics (claude code, codex, gemini cli) to confirm the Context7 servers are responding
- Do not hardcode library IDs; resolve them fresh each session since they can change

## 5. Report Findings

Summarize in a brief status block:
- Total active MCP servers and any failures
- Count of available tools, skills, agents, and commands
- Any configuration issues requiring attention
